1
00:00:00,325 --> 00:00:02,875
You tap a box that says
you're not a robot.

2
00:00:03,091 --> 00:00:06,716
Researchers say the test started
before your finger got there.

3
00:00:07,292 --> 00:00:10,442
For years, the test was
reading wobbly letters.

4
00:00:10,685 --> 00:00:15,025
In 2013, Google said its risk
analysis covers everything you do

5
00:00:15,035 --> 00:00:18,510
around a challenge, not
only the answer you type.

6
00:00:18,916 --> 00:00:24,066
In 2014, the checkbox arrived, and
many people passed with one click.

7
00:00:24,623 --> 00:00:26,488
The click feels like the test.

8
00:00:26,498 --> 00:00:28,748
It's really a request for a verdict.

9
00:00:29,046 --> 00:00:31,086
The evidence was gathered around it.

10
00:00:31,096 --> 00:00:33,061
If it looks human, you pass.

11
00:00:33,071 --> 00:00:35,296
If it can't tell, you get a puzzle.

12
00:00:35,918 --> 00:00:37,233
So what does it watch?

13
00:00:37,243 --> 00:00:39,468
Google doesn't publish the full list.

14
00:00:39,742 --> 00:00:44,257
A 2021 survey describes the checkbox
recording cursor movement,

15
00:00:44,267 --> 00:00:47,492
where you click, and how
long the pointer hovers.

16
00:00:47,732 --> 00:00:50,997
It also records browser
details and cookies.

17
00:00:51,007 --> 00:00:55,182
Google's own FAQ calls a cookie
essential to that analysis.

18
00:00:55,753 --> 00:00:58,903
A real hand never moves
at one steady speed.

19
00:00:59,054 --> 00:01:02,204
It speeds up, peaks,
and eases into the target.

20
00:01:02,510 --> 00:01:07,525
In 1985, Flash and Hogan modeled
arm movement this way: a smooth,

21
00:01:07,535 --> 00:01:09,435
bell-shaped curve of speed.

22
00:01:09,832 --> 00:01:14,332
One published detector checks cursor
paths against a model like that.

23
00:01:14,729 --> 00:01:19,594
In a 2024 study of reCAPTCHA
version two, any cursor movement

24
00:01:19,604 --> 00:01:22,104
cut the number of puzzles needed to pass.

25
00:01:22,414 --> 00:01:26,314
Straight paths and curved ones
were not significantly different.

26
00:01:26,637 --> 00:01:27,852
So is it the wobble?

27
00:01:27,862 --> 00:01:29,362
Google doesn't say.

28
00:01:29,950 --> 00:01:31,625
Then there's the browser.

29
00:01:31,848 --> 00:01:36,488
A 2024 study reports that cookies
and browsing history carry

30
00:01:36,498 --> 00:01:40,023
substantial weight in whether
a visitor seems human.

31
00:01:40,365 --> 00:01:43,205
Browsing history is
what researchers report.

32
00:01:43,215 --> 00:01:45,115
Google hasn't confirmed it.

33
00:01:45,716 --> 00:01:48,366
Version three has no checkbox at all.

34
00:01:48,564 --> 00:01:51,029
It returns a score from zero to one.

35
00:01:51,039 --> 00:01:53,629
One means very likely a good interaction.

36
00:01:53,639 --> 00:01:55,964
Zero means very likely a bot.

37
00:01:56,327 --> 00:02:01,352
Each site sets its own cutoff,
and Google suggests starting at 0.5.

38
00:02:01,691 --> 00:02:03,431
But a score can be pushed.

39
00:02:03,441 --> 00:02:07,706
In 2019, researchers trained an
AI agent to move and click in a

40
00:02:07,716 --> 00:02:09,266
simulator they built.

41
00:02:09,512 --> 00:02:14,227
It reached a score of 0.9,
about 97% of the time.

42
00:02:14,237 --> 00:02:16,612
The simulator isn't the live site.

43
00:02:17,188 --> 00:02:23,338
A 2023 study ran for thirteen
months with more than 3,600 people.

44
00:02:23,585 --> 00:02:28,275
Its authors judged the checkbox
costly, with close to no security gain,

45
00:02:28,285 --> 00:02:30,260
and say it should be retired.

46
00:02:30,812 --> 00:02:34,126
Nobody outside Google knows
how the signals are weighted.

47
00:02:34,136 --> 00:02:36,712
So every study tests from the outside.

48
00:02:37,316 --> 00:02:40,016
So the tap is the last step, not the test.

49
00:02:40,521 --> 00:02:42,296
It cares how you got there.
